# {{ design_name }} RTL project

This deterministic SystemVerilog 2017 project combines IP-XACT module
boundaries, instance hierarchy, and direct wire connections with stored
component/design SystemVerilog module-body semantics. Components without an
active body retain compile-safe leaf defaults, which are not functional implementations.
See implementation_status in generation-manifest.json. Every file is replaced on
regeneration.

## Verify

Install Verilator 5.x, GNU Make, and a C++ compiler, then run:

```bash
make lint
make smoke
```

The selected top module is `{{ top_module }}`.

## Smoke check

For a clocked check, supply the generated one-bit input IDs explicitly:

```bash
make smoke CLOCK=clk RESET=rst_n RESET_ACTIVE=0
```

Replace the example IDs with the IDs in the table below. No signal roles are
inferred. Omit RESET when the module has no reset. RESET_ACTIVE defaults to 0;
use 1 for an active-high reset. One clock and one reset are supported. With no
CLOCK, the check applies unclocked input patterns and does not verify sequential
behavior. Parameter defaults come from the Spec; parameter overrides are not
part of this check.

The generated `sim/smoke_tb.sv` exercises only the selected top module. It holds
reset for two cycles when supplied, applies 16 patterns (zero, all-one,
alternating-bit, then counters), and runs two final cycles with inputs held.
Each cycle takes 11 ns. Inputs change with the clock low. The clock rises after
5 ns and falls after another 5 ns; sampling occurs 1 ns later, after sequential
updates settle. Each sample prints its number, simulation time, phase, and all
port values in hexadecimal.

There are no expected values, output comparisons, or protocol transactions.
`Smoke run completed.` means that simulation completed, not that the RTL is
functionally correct. Unimplemented scaffolds can also complete this check.
Bidirectional ports are left undriven by the testbench. Verilator's two-state
simulation cannot establish unknown-value or high-impedance correctness.

